{
  "behaviorChanges": [
    "Implementation-review rounds are counted by EXECUTION per outgoing lineage (branch plus review base), not by the `--review-round` label. RCA 20260707T181643Z: six executions against one lineage, every one invoked as `R1`, each passing a label-derived cap as round 1 while the aggregate was round 6. TWO CURRENCIES, because one number cannot serve both comparisons: the absolute ceiling counts TOTAL executions -- all six of the incident's runs were confirming-by-predicate, so only quantity separates a pathology from a healthy remediation loop -- while the tier budget and the self-authorization rung count CHARGED executions, the confirming predicate replayed over the recorded sequence. Every free round stays free and the budget stays reachable. The recorded `branch` is verified rather than inferred from the filename slug -- `feat/a-b` and `feat/a_b` share one slug, so a fresh branch could otherwise spend another branch's budget. The confirming predicate reads the SAME ledger as the counters -- given a lineage base it scopes to that lineage -- so a branch carrying an old-base or void manifest no longer has its first new-lineage round admitted free and then replayed as charged; free going in and charged coming out is a budget bug in either direction. A void manifest is not counted as a spent execution, and an `--review-round` label the ladder would reject -- no digits at all, or a non-positive marker like `R0` -- reaches that refusal unchanged. Only a POSITIVE parsed marker is effective-counted; promoting the others would hand the ladder a positive number and silently retire a validation it already had.",
    "NOT WIP-safe. A branch that has already recorded rounds is now at the round its EXECUTIONS say, not the round its label says, so a lane mid-remediation -- and especially one that has been relabelling -- can meet the hard cap sooner than it expects on its very next run. The two exits are unchanged and neither needs another Codex execution: decompose into smaller PRs (a new branch is a new lineage and a fresh count), or, IF a recorded manifest still binds the diff being pushed, park the discretionary post-review changes and finalize on that manifest. Any base change -- rebase, retarget, a `review.baseRef` edit -- starts a new lineage and a fresh count. Adopt this release deliberately rather than by patch auto-update mid-review.",
    "`codex-run` prints `codex_run_execution_count:` on every run, label or not, and `codex_run_execution_warning:` when this attempt would be the third. It reports an ATTEMPT alongside the recorded and charged counts, not an execution: the line prints before the run is known to record anything, so a later refusal or a non-zero wrapper exit would otherwise have it claim a spend that never happened. Stage 2 manifest filenames carry microseconds -- separated so the time-of-day and the microseconds never run together into the twelve consecutive digits the public-release scan reads as an account-like identifier, since these filenames are recorded INTO the tracked ledger. The manifest and the log it pins share ONE stamp definition -- two executions inside one second used to collide, and fixing only the manifest would make the first execution's evidence permanently unfinalizable."
  ],
